just rtest with a name filter reports ok on 0 passed and 0 failed: a filter that matches nothing is a wall of green for a run that executed nothing

Found by lane-resolver during B145: `just rtest b145` printed `ok` for every
binary, with `0 passed; 0 failed` on each, because cargo filters on TEST NAMES
and no test name contained the string "b145". The lane had just written the
tests. Nothing ran, and the output was indistinguishable from success.

This is the most dangerous shape a gate can have, because it fails in the
direction of confidence and it fails SILENTLY at exactly the moment a lane is
most inclined to believe it: right after writing the tests it wants to see pass.
A lane that ran only this and reported green would have reported a green for
zero executed assertions, in good faith.

WHY IT IS NOT JUST CARGO BEING CARGO
  Bare `cargo test FILTER` has always behaved this way and we cannot change that.
  But `just rtest` is OUR wrapper, it already parses per-binary totals to produce
  the SUITE-VERIFIED line, and it is the thing every lane in this batch is told
  to run. The wrapper has the numbers in hand and chooses to print ok anyway.

WHAT TO DO
  When a name filter is supplied and the run executes ZERO tests across ALL
  binaries, exit non-zero and say so in the words that name the cause — that the
  filter matched no test name, not that the tests failed. Zero-in-one-binary is
  normal and must stay quiet; zero across every binary with a filter present is
  the bug. With no filter, zero total is already impossible and needs no rule.

  Check whether `rcheck` / `rclippy` / `rclippy-delta` have the same shape when
  given an argument, and whether the unfiltered path can ever reach zero (e.g. a
  compile that produces no binaries). Do not assume the filtered path is the only
  one — measure it.

REQUIRED EVIDENCE, not a description of it
  - RED: on current tooling, `just rtest <string-matching-nothing>` prints ok.
    Capture it.
  - GREEN: after the fix, the same invocation exits non-zero with a message
    naming the filter.
  - POSITIVE CONTROL: `just rtest b129` (a filter that DOES match) still passes
    and still prints its real totals. Without this the fix could be "always fail
    when a filter is given" and the red/green would look identical.
  - And the unfiltered full run is unchanged: 0 diffs in the SUITE-VERIFIED line.

Related doctrine already recorded in docs/verification-hazards.md: "A control
proves nothing by not failing", and "A check that keeps passing while you loosen
it is measuring something else". This is their tooling-level cousin and belongs
in that document too once fixed.